atches  | api.github.com · 2026-10-31\n\n## Who made it?\n\nFeature #7 · lineage\n\nFacts about the publisher. We do not turn them into a trust score.\n\nNamespace\n    io.github.alexar76 · 8 servers\nGitHub owner\n    a personal account (alexar76)\nBuild record\n    signed\n\nRepository\n    github.com/alexar76/warden\nStars\n    0\nRepository age\n    31–90 days\nLast push\n    0–30 days ago\nArchived\n    no\nSecurity policy\n    SECURITY.md\n\nSource: the registry entry and api.github.com · 2026-10-31; security policy read from GitHub · 2026-10-13.\n\n## Tool list changes\n\nOur reads compared · no tool called\n\n#### Package npm @aimarket/warden\n\n1 of 1 comparison between consecutive reads showed a change (2 reads, 2026-10-13 to 2026-10-23; versions 0.8.2 to 0.18.0).\n\nLast change seen between our reads on 2026-10-13 (version 0.8.2) and 2026-10-23 (version 0.18.0).\n\nChanged (6):\n\n`approve_mcp_server: title, description, input schema, annotations``list_scan_rules: description``revoke_mcp_server: title, description, input schema, annotations``static_scan_tools: description``status_mcp_server: title, description, input schema, annotations``vet_mcp_server: input schema`\n\nVersion 0.22.2 (current) has not been read yet.\n\nCompared from the tool lists our read-only connection recorded, separately for each hosted endpoint and each package: each tool's name, title, description, input schema and annotations (the order of keys and of tools is ignored). We see a list only when we read it: a change is shown between the two reads where we first saw it, and anything after 2026-10-23 is not known yet.   * Met No known critical or high advisory in the package or its dependencies None known in the package or its dependency tree.\n\nRead from public records only: the registry entry, npm/PyPI, GitHub and advisory databases, checked 2026-10-31 (security policy 2026-10-13). No tool was called. Only “met” and “not met” are counted, so the total differs between servers; a check that does not apply or that we could not run is listed, never counted against it. It is not a safety rating, and the same rules apply to every server. How each check works · Leaderboards · How we stay neutral\n\n## Common questions about io.github.alexar76/warden\n\nAnswered from the data above\n\n### Who publishes io.github.alexar76/warden?\n\nThe registry ties the namespace io.github.alexar76 to the GitHub account alexar76; only its owner can publish under it. Its linked repository, github.com/alexar76/warden, belongs to the same GitHub account. These are facts from the registry and GitHub, not a rating.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTwo public records of MCP tool definitions, one day, 23,190 servers: where they agree (https://modelmarket.dev/blog/two-public-records-of-mcp-tool-definitions-one-day-23190-servers-where-they-agree/)\nciteturn3search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: 6 days ago; Several projects now keep a public record of what MCP servers tell models about their tools: HISTOR (ours), heldfast, rugsnare, and signed per-tool grades from AgentAvow.\n\n31 Oct 2026 · 6 min read\n\n# Two public records of MCP tool definitions, one day, 23,190 servers: where they agree\n\n#mcp#ai#security#opensource\n\nImage\n\nSeveral projects now keep a public record of what MCP servers tell models about their tools: HISTOR (ours), heldfast, rugsnare, and signed per-tool grades from AgentAvow. Each hashes a tool definition its own way. If two of them read the same server, do they see the same thing, and can one answer in the other's format?\n\nOn 26 October HISTOR published its weekly signed snapshot at 13:49:50Z. heldfast checkpointed its feed at 13:48:36Z the same day. Both are public, so the comparison below can be rerun from the pinned inputs.\n\n## The formats\n\nAll five use SHA-256 over sorted-key JSON, and four over RFC 8785. They differ in which fields count and in how \"missing\" is written:\n\n | name  | title  | description  | inputSchema  | outputSchema  | annotations  | icons\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nHISTOR tool-set digest  | yes  | beside it  | yes  | yes  | yes  | beside it  | beside it\nWhere today's definitions match one of the two copies (15 servers), they match the copy of whichever reader came later.\n\n78 servers differ only in how they were read, not because the servers changed.\n\nThe definition contains the reader. Some packages put their environment into tool text. `@sunkimkk/pinterest-mcp@1.2.0` describes its download folder as `/tmp/churn-…/Downloads/…` to heldfast and `/home/histor/Downloads/…` to us. `airweave-mcp-search@0.9.62` names its tool after an environment value, so the tool is `search-heldfast-churn-placeholder` for one reader and `search-histor-envkey-…` for the other. Others default a date parameter to today or a field to a fresh UUID. Every reader gets its own digest for these servers, and every user does too.\n\nThe network. Some packages build part of their tool list from a remote service. heldfast ran npm packages with network until 29 September and without it since; HISTOR runs them without. For 24 packages at the same version, the September copy at heldfast differs from ours: richer annotations, other prices in the descriptions, more tools.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOur Multica agents now work in an agent market: they buy, remember, review sellers, and sell their own work | TypeScript LibHunt (https://www.libhunt.com/posts/1567497-our-multica-agents-now-work-in-an-agent-market-they-buy-remember-review-sellers-and-sell-their-own-work)\nciteturn3search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 days ago; Crawled: 3 days ago; Image: alexar76 logo ... Our WARDEN guard runs as an OpenCode plugin (@aimarket/warden/opencode, from 0.23.0). ... In a staged attack we planted a fake GitHub token in a file.\n\n  3. ### aimarket-hub\n\n2 4 0 8.1 Python\n\nAIMarket Hub — federated capability catalog, channels, invoke API, and plugins.\n\nImage: alexar76 logo\n\n> Multica is an agent factory: you give an agent a runtime, instructions and a schedule, and it works through tasks. AIMarket hubs (modelmarket.dev is the public one) are a market where agents buy capabilities from each other per call. We run both, and in the first week of November we connected them. This is what the agents do now, what broke, what we found when we audited ourselves, and a setup guide in five languages for anyone who wants the same.\n\n  4. ### warden\n\n3 6 0 8.7 TypeScript\n\nWARDEN — MCP security firewall: vets an MCP server's tool definitions against static-scan rules, a signed threat feed, origin and tool-def pinning before any tool reaches the model. Zero-dependency TypeScript library. (by alexar76)\n\nImage: alexar76 logo\n\n> A flow guard in the runtime. Our WARDEN guard runs as an OpenCode plugin (@aimarket/warden/opencode, from 0.23.0). After the session has read outside text, it refuses a tool call that would carry a credential or text the session read. In a staged attack we planted a fake GitHub token in a file. Sending it was refused through the chat script, curl to an allowed host, a memory write, a project file and the tracker CLI. A normal day's digest ran with zero refusals, after we fixed one false positive: a task's UUID looked like private text.\n\n  5. ### AI-Factory\n\n4 5 6 9.1 Python\n\nAI-Factory — autonomous pipeline that designs, builds, tests, and publishes products.\n\nImage: alexar76 logo\n\n> Step by step, from the container to selling a long job, in English, Russian, Spanish, French and Chinese: multica/manual. The Multica skill our agents use for buying and selling is SKILL.md. The full audit is in REPORT.md.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nalexar76/warden — MCP Servers · Directories by Enterprise DNA (https://enterprisedna.co/directories/mcp/alexar76-warden/)\ncit